  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">EAN- Mines to School</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">From Mines to School is an action that intends to reduce and prevent worst forms of child labour in the mining communities of the disadvantaged districts of Moroto, Nakapiripirit and Amudat in the Karamoja sub-region. Building on previous experiences of the consortium members in the sub-region and work on similar projects, the action will focus on two main strategies; strengthening the capacity of duty bearers and grass-root Civil Society Organisations (CSOs) to reduce and prevent child labour and propose alternative waysof livelihoods and education opportunities for affected children, their parents, and guardians. The action will also support lower-level administrators and grass-root CSOs to enforce laws, policies ordinances and by-laws prohibiting children from working in the mines and identify child protection issues and protection violations. It will liaise with the key relevant ministries and work with members of Parliament to influence the implementation of the just enacted Mining and Minerals Act2022, address problematic issues, including violation of human rights for the artisanal miners, and children working in the mines, request for an increase in the budgetallocation for the child labour response, ensure that a central coordination authorityis present at district level and community level and ensure that  child labour committees are functional at village and parish level. Additionally, the action will link childrenwithdrawn from labour to professional counselling and legal aid if required,set up mobile learning centres near the mines, offer alternative education, and ensure linkages to scholarship programs. The action will also establish income-generating activities targeting vulnerable parents and guardians of victims of child labour specifically single mothers, widows, women with disabilities, and the elderly. Linkages to existing livelihood programs such as the resilience program under Welthungerhilfe Uganda, Mercy Corps, and relevant government programs such as the  Parish DevelopmentModel will be established and implemented. The action will adopt a human rights approach to promote gender mainstreaming through puppet theatres and the innovative.  GALS Methodology with the aim of addressing gender inequalities, social norms, traditions, and cultures that promote children especially girls working in the mines to provide for their families.</narrative>
  </description>
  <participating-org role="4" type="24">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">EDUCATION ADVOCACY NETWORK LTD</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Education Advocacy Network was first established as a lose network of member organizations championing the cause for education advocacy campaigning and stakeholder sensitisation in 2019. Through its advocacy efforts with the Ministry of Education and Sports, Ministry of Gender, Labour and Social Development and Ministry of Local Government, it was prudent that the status of the network transforms from being a lose platform to a fully registered NGO. Based on the recommendations by member organisations including UWEZO Uganda, FAWE Uganda, Rafiki Milele, Youth Line Forum, Niyetu and the Parliamentary Forum on Youth among other individual and institutions as members, the network fully registered with the National Bureau for NGOs. It is now fully registered and spanning its fifth yearof continued education advocacy at all levels namely, local, national, sub-regional and regional levels.

The mission of Education Advocacy Network is &#x201c;to promote effective and reflective education advocacy that positively influences education policy and practiceat all levels of the country&#x2019;s education system.&#x201d;

Institutional objectives: As documented in the Strategic Plan, there are four objectives:

1. Ensure timely, relevant and quality advocacy efforts in the education sector;
2. Promote effective coordination of advocacy efforts to support community engagement at all levels;
3. Promote a stakeholder friendly environment for meaningful participation and engagement in all education processes at all levels;
4. Promote the development of a well-informed and resourced cadre of education advocates at local and national level.
The organization was registered to operate national wide in all the districts of Uganda. The organization works in all geographic areas of the country for it is national in operation. The key thematic program focus areas include, Basic and secondary education, Education Policy Advocacy, Membership growth and strengthening, community engagement andadvocacy as well as research and documentation.

The organization is legally registered and recognised as a contributor to the Ministry of Education and Sports vision, mission, goal and objectives. As a membership organisation its trust and credibility is guaranteed by the ministry of education and sports through continuous engagements and invitations it gets to participate in a number of itsprograms and activities. The organisation has thematic members whose work is structured according to the structures of the Ministryof education and sports.</narrative>
  </participating-org>
